//? Title: Kernel Ridge Regression: Centring, Shrinkage and Validation
//? Subtitle: Exact two-point fits, signed prediction weights and fold-local preprocessing
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A regression curve must predict a numeric response from inputs whose relationship
may be nonlinear, while controlling how closely it follows noisy observations.
Kernel ridge regression fits a penalised function through similarities between
training rows. We derive the free-intercept estimator and check every quantity on
two observations. A four-row counterexample separates fixed-metric leave-one-out
from validation that refits preprocessing. A noisy sine wave tests spectral shrinkage
against a known mean; Iris supplies a separate, untouched regression holdout.
The examples distinguish training fit, tuning scores and evaluation error.
)
\toc

> The method

>> Similarities define a function space

Given inputs \(x_i\) and numeric responses \(y_i\), choose a positive-semidefinite
kernel \(k\) and its reproducing-kernel Hilbert space \(\mathcal H\). Fit a scalar
intercept \(b\) and a function \(f\) by

$$
 \min_{b\in\mathbb R,\,f\in\mathcal H}\ \sum_{i=1}^n[y_i-b-f(x_i)]^2+\lambda\|f\|_{\mathcal H}^2,
 \qquad f(x)=\sum_{i=1}^n\alpha_i k(x_i,x),\qquad K_{ij}=k(x_i,x_j).
$$
\alttext(Minimise the sum of squared response residuals plus lambda times the squared function norm, leaving the intercept unpenalised. The representer form expresses the function as a weighted sum of kernels anchored at the training rows.)

The representer form follows by decomposing any function into its projection onto
the span of training kernels and an orthogonal remainder. The remainder evaluates
to zero at every training input, so removing it preserves the loss and reduces the
penalty. For \(\lambda>0\), a minimum therefore lies in that finite span.
The library uses a **sum** of losses: a mean-loss penalty \(\rho\) corresponds to
\(\lambda=n\rho\). Changing that convention changes numerical penalty values.

Supported kernels are linear \(x^Tz\), polynomial \((\gamma x^Tz+c)^d\), and RBF
\(\exp[-\gamma\|x-z\|^2]\). Polynomial degree is a positive integer, offset is
nonnegative, and nonlinear gamma is positive. By default each resolved Formula
feature uses its training mean and population SD; a constant feature uses divisor
one. Thus gamma measures distance in those standardised coordinates. RBF length
scale \(\ell\) in the convention \(\exp[-\|x-z\|^2/(2\ell^2)]\) gives
\(\gamma=1/(2\ell^2)\). A large gamma makes similarities local; it does not by
itself specify the fitted complexity, which also depends on lambda and the data.

>> A free intercept requires kernel centring

Let \(C=I-\mathbf1\mathbf1^T/n\), \(K_c=CKC\), and \(y_c=Cy\). For positive
lambda, the intercept normal equation and kernel equations give

$$
 \begin{bmatrix}K+\lambda I&\mathbf1\\\mathbf1^T&0\end{bmatrix}
 \begin{bmatrix}\alpha\\b\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}y\\0\end{bmatrix},
 \quad\alpha=(K_c+\lambda I)^{-1}y_c,
 \quad b=\bar y-\bar k^T\alpha,\quad \bar k=K\mathbf1/n.
$$
\alttext(The augmented kernel system enforces zero sum of dual coefficients. Equivalently solve the doubly centred kernel system on centred responses. The raw-kernel intercept is response mean minus the training kernel-column means dotted with alpha.)

Merely subtracting \(\bar y\) before solving an *uncentred* kernel generally fails
these equations: the dual coefficients need not sum to zero. A query's centred
kernel row is \(k_{*c,j}=k(x_*,x_j)-\bar k_j-\bar k_*+\bar K\), where
\(\bar k_*\) averages its training similarities and \(\bar K\) averages the full
training Gram. Prediction is \(\bar y+k_{*c}^T\alpha=b+k_*^T\alpha\).
Every centring quantity comes from the fitted rows. The option `fit_intercept=false`
uses raw kernels and raw responses with zero intercept. Formula `+1`/`-1` markers
are suppressed in the feature design; this option controls the intercept.

The retained diagram needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm to rebuild.
Documentation exports consume its saved local SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/krr_centred_shrinkage.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/krr_centred_shrinkage.tikz > build/krr_centred_shrinkage.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/krr_centred_shrinkage.svg build/krr_centred_shrinkage.dvi > build/krr_centred_shrinkage.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the free-intercept and spectral-shrinkage diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the free-intercept and spectral-shrinkage diagram.
// \image(figures/krr_centred_shrinkage.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The constant response direction is preserved by the free intercept; centred kernel directions are shrunk.)
\alttext(Split the response into its constant mean and centred component. The mean passes unchanged to predictions. Each centred kernel eigenvector component is multiplied by eigenvalue divided by eigenvalue plus lambda, then the parts are added.)

>> Eigenvalues explain effective complexity

Write \(K_c=U\operatorname{diag}(d_j)U^T\) on the centred subspace. Each response
coordinate \(u_j^Ty_c\) is multiplied by \(d_j/(d_j+\lambda)\). Hence

$$
 \widehat y=Sy,\quad
 S=\mathbf1\mathbf1^T/n+U\operatorname{diag}\left(\frac{d_j}{d_j+\lambda}\right)U^T,
 \quad\mathrm{df}=\operatorname{tr}S=1+\sum_{d_j>0}\frac{d_j}{d_j+\lambda},
 \quad\frac{\partial\mathrm{df}}{\partial\lambda}=-\sum_{d_j>0}\frac{d_j}{(d_j+\lambda)^2}\le0.
$$
\alttext(The smoother preserves the constant direction and shrinks each positive centred-kernel direction. Its trace is one plus the sum of shrinkage factors, whose derivative with respect to lambda is nonpositive.)

For a fixed kernel, df tends to one as the penalty grows; without an intercept the
constant term is absent. Larger lambda reduces the fitted RKHS norm and increases
training residual sum of squares. This also follows by adding the optimality
inequalities for two penalties. Neither fact proves monotone validation or truth
error. A free-intercept prediction has weights summing to one, so constant responses
are reproduced; weights can be negative and predictions can exceed the observed
response range. These are penalised function fits, unlike positive local averages.

At zero penalty, the implementation uses a spectral pseudoinverse with eigenvalues
at or below \(64n\epsilon\max_{ij}|K_{ij}|\) treated as numerical zero. It chooses
the minimum-RKHS-norm least-squares limit under that rank convention. Contradictory
responses at duplicate inputs cannot be interpolated. Tiny positive penalties can
still be ill-conditioned. The diagnostics expose rank, objective and equation
residual; at zero penalty with incompatible labels, an equation residual need not
vanish because the centred response can lie outside the kernel range.

>> Three different errors and two validation procedures

Training predictions use all fitting responses. `fitted_values()`, `rmse()` and
`r_squared()` retain this API's historical meaning: **fixed-metric LOO** predictions
and scores. With fixed positive lambda, define

$$
 B=C(K_c+\lambda I)^{-1}C,\qquad
 \widehat y_i^{(-i)}=y_i-\frac{\alpha_i}{B_{ii}},\qquad
 \operatorname{LOO}_{\mathrm{metric}}=\frac1n\sum_i(y_i-\widehat y_i^{(-i)})^2.
$$
\alttext(The fixed-metric deletion prediction subtracts alpha i divided by the corresponding diagonal of the centred inverse from response i. Average its squared residuals for the fixed-metric LOO score.)

Here the deleted fit refits the intercept and kernel centring but freezes the
full-data predictor scaling, resolved feature schema, gamma and lambda. Without an
intercept use \(C=I\). This is the usual residual-over-one-minus-leverage identity,
computed through the inverse diagonal to avoid cancellation when leverage is near
one. At lambda zero the code explicitly fits deleted Gram matrices; it does not
replace a zero denominator with an arbitrary floor.

`kernel_ridge_cross_validate` instead fits each fold's Formula preprocessing,
predictor scaling, response offset and kernel centring using that fold's training
rows. It requires explicit, fixed candidates, contiguous retained fold IDs and at
least two fitting rows per fold. Its pooled MSE weights validation rows equally.
Returned fold means/scales and row-aligned predictions make this distinction
inspectable. Candidate ties choose the first strict minimum. An unavailable fold
schema or invalid held-out prediction raises an error rather than dropping it.
Neither a minimum tuning score nor the score used to select gamma estimates the
selected procedure's generalisation error without an outer holdout or nested CV.

Default automatic fitting searches 40 log-spaced lambdas from 0.0001 to 1000 and
12 gammas from 0.03 to 30 times the inverse upper-median *positive* squared pair
distance in the full training metric. Constant metrics use base one. Explicit
candidate vectors preserve caller order. This finite search minimises the
fixed-metric LOO score; it is neither continuous optimisation nor fold-refitted CV.

Dense work for G gammas and L positive penalties is
\(O(G(n^2p+n^3+Ln^2))\), with \(O(n^2+np)\) fitted storage. Zero-penalty deletion
refits can add \(O(Gn^4)\). A GP with the same fixed mean, kernel and noise-to-signal
ratio lambda has the corresponding posterior mean. Free-intercept treatment must
also agree; empirical response centring alone is a different mean convention.
KRR supplies no posterior predictive variance.

> Small worked examples

>> Two RBF anchors: all the numbers fit on a page

Use \(x=(0,1)\), \(y=(0,2)\), no predictor standardisation,
\(\gamma=\log2\), and \(\lambda=1/2\). Then
\(K=\left[\begin{smallmatrix}1&1/2\\1/2&1\end{smallmatrix}\right]\),
\(K_c=\tfrac14\left[\begin{smallmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{smallmatrix}\right]\),
and \(y_c=(-1,1)^T\). The centred contrast eigenvalue is 1/2, so its shrinkage is
1/2. Thus \(\alpha=(-1,1)\), \(b=1\), training predictions are \((1/2,3/2)\),
\(S=\left[\begin{smallmatrix}3/4&1/4\\1/4&3/4\end{smallmatrix}\right]\),
and df is 1.5. Each deletion leaves a single observation whose free intercept
reproduces that response: LOO predictions are \((2,0)\), RMSE is two, and LOO
R-squared is minus three. Negative R-squared is legitimate. The midpoint prediction
is one by symmetry. Training RSS is 1/2, squared RKHS norm is one, and objective is one.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/krr_two_point.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{0, 1});
  d.add_column("y", std::vector<double>{0, 2});
  dm::stats::KernelRidgeRegressionOptions o;
  o.lambda      = .5;
  o.gamma       = std::log(2.);
  o.standardize = false;
  const dm::stats::KernelRidgeRegression m(d, "y ~ x", o);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame                 q;
  q.add_column("x", dm::seql(-1, 2, 201).values());
  const auto          x = q.numeric_column("x").values();
  const auto          w = m.prediction_weights(q);
  std::vector<double> w0, w1;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.size(); ++i) {
    w0.push_back(w(i, 0));
    w1.push_back(w(i, 1));
  }
  auto fit =
      dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
          .line(x, m.predict(q), "fit", {40, 100, 190})
          .points({0, 1}, {0, 2}, "observed", {30, 30, 30}, 4)
          .points({0, 1}, m.training_predictions(), "training", {190, 100, 35}, 4)
          .title("Two RBF anchors")
          .x_label("x")
          .y_label("response")
          .x_limits(-1, 2)
          .y_limits(-.5, 3)
          .legend({"fit", "observed", "training"}, {{40, 100, 190}, {30, 30, 30}, {190, 100, 35}});
  auto weights = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                     .line(x, w0, "row 0", {40, 100, 190})
                     .line(x, w1, "row 1", {190, 100, 35})
                     .title("Response weights sum to one")
                     .x_label("query x")
                     .y_label("prediction weight")
                     .x_limits(-1, 2)
                     .y_limits(-.15, 1.5)
                     .legend({"row 0", "row 1"}, {{40, 100, 190}, {190, 100, 35}});
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(fit).add(weights).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/krr_two_point.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n\n{}\n\n{}\n",
      m.training_diagnostics().to_md(6),
      m.spectrum().to_md(6),
      m.diagnostics()
          .select(
              {"df",
               "training_rmse",
               "loo_rmse",
               "rkhs_norm_squared",
               "objective",
               "system_residual"})
          .to_md(6));
}
```
