\image(figures/krr_two_point.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The fitted contrast is half the observed contrast; deletion error is much larger than training error.)
\alttext(The RBF fit passes through training fitted values 0.5 and 1.5. Query weights vary smoothly and add to one; both the fit and weights return toward equal weighting away from the anchors.)

>> Linear kernel: ridge in feature coordinates, with signed extrapolation weights

For a linear kernel the representer form gives \(f(x)=x^T\beta\) with
\(\beta=X_c^T\alpha=(X_c^TX_c+\lambda I)^{-1}X_c^Ty_c\).
The first equality and the centred kernel system prove the second by multiplying
through \(X_c^T\); uniqueness follows for positive lambda. With the same two rows,
\(X_c^TX_c=1/2\) and \(X_c^Ty_c=1\), so \(\beta=1\), \(b=1/2\).
At query \(x=3\), prediction is 3.5, and its response weights are
\((-3/4,7/4)\). They sum to one but are not probabilities. For constant responses
seven, the prediction is seven at every query. The figure includes the extrapolation
region explicitly rather than implying interpolation is all the model can do.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/krr_linear_weights.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{0, 1});
  d.add_column("y", std::vector<double>{0, 2});
  dm::stats::KernelRidgeRegressionOptions o;
  o.kernel      = "linear";
  o.lambda      = .5;
  o.standardize = false;
  const dm::stats::KernelRidgeRegression m(d, "y ~ x", o);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame                 q;
  q.add_column("x", dm::seql(-1, 3, 101).values());
  const auto          x = q.numeric_column("x").values();
  const auto          w = m.prediction_weights(q);
  std::vector<double> a, b;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.size(); ++i) {
    a.push_back(w(i, 0));
    b.push_back(w(i, 1));
  }
  auto line =
      dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
          .line(x, m.predict(q), "ridge fit", {40, 100, 190})
          .points({0, 1}, {0, 2}, "observed", {30, 30, 30}, 4)
          .points({3}, {3.5}, "query", {190, 100, 35}, 4)
          .title("Linear-kernel extrapolation")
          .x_label("x")
          .y_label("response")
          .x_limits(-1, 3)
          .y_limits(-1, 5)
          .legend(
              {"ridge fit", "observed", "query"}, {{40, 100, 190}, {30, 30, 30}, {190, 100, 35}});
  auto weights = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                     .line(x, a, "row 0", {40, 100, 190})
                     .line(x, b, "row 1", {190, 100, 35})
                     .abline_h(0, {130, 130, 130}, 1)
                     .title("Signed weights are not probabilities")
                     .x_label("query x")
                     .y_label("prediction weight")
                     .x_limits(-1, 3)
                     .y_limits(-1, 2.7)
                     .legend({"row 0", "row 1"}, {{40, 100, 190}, {190, 100, 35}});
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(line).add(weights).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/krr_linear_weights.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame query;
  query.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{3});
  const auto                             qw = m.prediction_weights(query);
  const dm::stats::KernelRidgeRegression constant(
      d.mutate("y", std::vector<double>{7, 7}), "y ~ x", o);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame out;
  out.add_column("query_x", std::vector<double>{3});
  out.add_column("weight_0", std::vector<double>{qw(0, 0)});
  out.add_column("weight_1", std::vector<double>{qw(0, 1)});
  out.add_column("prediction", m.predict(query));
  out.add_column("constant_response_prediction", constant.predict(query));
  std::print("{}\n", out.to_md(6));
}
```
