\caption(Refitting predictor scaling changes each deletion prediction and its aggregate score.)
\alttext(Four observed responses are compared with frozen-metric and refitted-metric LOO predictions. The fold omitting the remote x value learns a much smaller training SD; the fold means and SDs are displayed separately.)

> Checking the theory on a known function

>> A noisy sine wave: tuning targets observations, evaluation targets the known mean

`kernel_ridge_wave` preserves the original experiment: 120 uniform inputs on [0,1],
responses \(y=\sin(2\pi x)+\epsilon\), independent normal noise SD 0.15,
and local seed 20240815. The C++ standard distributions can differ across standard
libraries, so exact reproducibility refers to this build's generated rows.
We retain all rows for fitting and use the default finite fixed-metric search.
The 401-point evaluation grid has response equal to the generating *mean*, not a
fresh noisy observation. Its RMSE measures curve recovery; it is not a holdout
estimate of noisy-response prediction error. In particular the noise SD is not a
lower bound on error against the noiseless mean.

The selected gamma is about 15.6026 and lambda 0.170125, giving df 18.5290.
Training RMSE is about 0.12535 and conditional LOO RMSE 0.14734. This corrected
free-intercept fit is fairly local: its mean-curve error is about 0.06, despite a
small tuning score. The displayed curve and independent reference calculation are
more informative than claiming that cross-validation recovers the true function
optimally. The tuning curve below holds gamma at its selected value; it is one
slice of the full 480-candidate search.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/krr_wave.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto                             d = dm::stats::kernel_ridge_wave();
  const dm::stats::KernelRidgeRegression m(d, "y ~ x");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame                 truth;
  truth.add_column("x", dm::seql(0, 1, 401).values());
  truth = truth.mutate_with(
      "y", [](const auto& r) { return std::sin(2 * std::numbers::pi * r.get_double("x")); });
  const auto x = truth.numeric_column("x").values();
  auto       fit =
      dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
          .points(
              d.numeric_column("x").values(),
              d.numeric_column("y").values(),
              "observed",
              {130, 130, 130},
              2)
          .line(x, truth.numeric_column("y").values(), "mean", {190, 100, 35})
          .line(x, m.predict(truth), "KRR", {40, 100, 190})
          .title("Noisy sine and selected fit")
          .x_label("x")
          .y_label("response")
          .x_limits(0, 1)
          .y_limits(-1.6, 2)
          .legend({"observed", "mean", "KRR"}, {{130, 130, 130}, {190, 100, 35}, {40, 100, 190}});
  auto curve = m.plot_cv_curve();
  curve.title("Selected gamma: conditional LOO");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(fit).add(curve).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/krr_wave.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n\n{}\n\nMean-curve grid score\n\n{}\n",
      m.selection_table()
          .filter([](const auto& r) { return r.get_double("selected") == 1; })
          .to_md(6),
      m.diagnostics()
          .select(
              {"intercept",
               "rank",
               "df",
               "training_rmse",
               "loo_rmse",
               "rkhs_norm_squared",
               "objective"})
          .to_md(6),
      m.score(truth).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| candidate | gamma | lambda | conditional_loo_mse | df | selected |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 418 | 15.602620 | 0.170125 | 0.021709 | 18.529039 | 1 |


