> What the calculations establish

The two-point examples recover the augmented normal equations, centred-kernel
shrinkage, full smoother trace and deletion predictions exactly. Signed weights
explain linear extrapolation while preserving constant responses. The four-row
example demonstrates that exact fixed-metric LOO is a different question from
refitting the whole preprocessing pipeline. The sine path checks monotone
complexity and norm reduction without claiming monotone evaluation error.
The Iris exercise reserves evaluation rows before fitting and compares against a
training-only baseline. Its single deterministic split supports a limited
predictive comparison, not population confidence intervals or a universal choice
of kernel.

Required-null fitting rows are omitted jointly; retained nonfinite values are
rejected. Prediction preserves query-row order, returning NaN for invalid required
query cells. `score` requires every evaluation response and prediction to be finite,
and refuses empty or partially scored sets. Dense kernels limit scale; categorical
expansions and a chosen distance metric can dominate the result. Gamma and lambda
are model choices whose uncertainty is absent from the reported point predictions.

> References

- G. S. Kimeldorf and G. Wahba (1971), “Some results on Tchebycheffian spline
  functions”, *Journal of Mathematical Analysis and Applications* 33(1), 82–95.
  [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-247X(71)90184-3).
  The finite representer argument connects penalised function fitting to kernels.
- C. Saunders, A. Gammerman and V. Vovk (1998), “Ridge Regression Learning
  Algorithm in Dual Variables”, *Proceedings of ICML*, 515–521.
  [Author-repository record](https://eprints.soton.ac.uk/258942/) and
  [paper](https://eprints.soton.ac.uk/258942/1/Dualrr_ICML98.pdf).
  This report makes its optional free-intercept centring and sum-loss convention explicit.

